  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nMid-level ridge will continue to cause persistent heat through the\nend of the week. On Friday, there remains uncertainty in the \ncoverage of debris clouds from morning convection to our north.\nDespite this, after collaboration with surrounding offices, have\nopted to extend the heat advisory south of Interstate 70 to now \ninclude Friday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nShortwave moving east through the Great Lakes will act to compress\nthe northern reaches of the mid-level ridge on Thursday. With mid-\nlayer CAPE around 2,000J/kg and effective shear approaching 25 knots\nacross central Ohio, a few severe storms are possible. Timing varies\nacross the CAMs, with NAM Nest and HRRR late in the evening, and the\nARW in the late afternoon. This results in a lower confidence\nforecast.\n\nBehind Friday's shortwave, mid-level flow becomes more northwesterly,\nwith several disturbances moving through the flow. With quite a bit\nof instability in place and temperatures aloft becoming slightly\ncooler, the chances for storms increases a bit. However, continued\ntiming uncertainty makes for a difficult forecast. At this time,\nstill think the best chance for convection will be Friday morning\nwith a weakening convective system, and again during the\nafternoon/evening as a weak front slides into our CWA from the north.\n\nIt appears additional showers and storms are possible Saturday before\nsurface high pressure builds in and",
